CHMOD(1)                       User Commands                        CHMOD(1)

NAME
    chmod - change a file's permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod MODE PATH

DESCRIPTION
    Set the permission bits of PATH to MODE, which is octal, one to four
    digits (for example 644 or 0755). There is no symbolic form, and a
    mode that does not parse is refused rather than partly applied.

THROUGH A CAPABILITY
    In a session, who may reach a file is the filesystem policy's to say,
    not the mode bits', so chmod stores nothing. It succeeds when it asks
    for what is already true: no set-id or sticky bit, group bits zero,
    other bits exactly what stat(1) reports for everyone else, and owner
    bits among your own rights. Otherwise it is refused. So chmod 600 on
    a file another account can also reach fails, and that is how you
    learn the file is shared.

SECURITY
    Without a session, the owner of a file may change its mode, and so
    may the administrator. The fs_server decides, against the identity
    the kernel attests for your login, never against anything the shell
    says.

SEE ALSO
    stat(1), chown(8)
